// @ens-test
// @exit 0
// @stdout PASS metadata reports a file's own length and nothing beside it
// @stdout PASS metadata reports an empty file as empty rather than as unreadable
// @stdout PASS metadata reports a directory with no length of its own
// @stdout PASS metadata reports a modification time inside a plausible window
// @stdout PASS a modification time moves when the content changes
// @stdout PASS a link is described both as itself and as what it points to
// @stdout PASS metadata reports why it could not be read
// @stdout PASS a path leading through a file is reported in each platform's own terms
// @stdout PASS the real path resolves the dots a path is written with
// @stdout PASS the real path reports how much room it requires when it is given none
// @stdout PASS the real path reports why there is none
// @stdout PASS the current directory is a directory this program can read
// @stdout PASS a number no file call reports classifies as Other
// @stdout PASS a file written through the wide open reads back byte for byte
// @stdout PASS an open at the end adds to what is already there
// @stdout PASS an open from empty drops what was there
// @stdout PASS an open that must create refuses a path that is already there
// @stdout PASS an open to read reports why there is nothing to read
// @stdout PASS an open is refused a directory in every mode and in each platform's own terms
// @stdout PASS creating a directory that is already there is reported
// @stdout PASS removing a file that is not there is reported
// @stdout PASS removing a directory that still holds something reports it
// @stdout PASS a rename refuses a destination that is taken and replaces one with the replace flag
// @stdout PASS a rename into a free name needs no replacing
// @stdout PASS a folder lists what it holds with each entry's kind
// @stdout PASS a folder with nothing in it lists nothing
// @stdout PASS a folder read where a file is reports it
// @stdout PASS a file the bridge creates is not executable
// @stdout PASS a file opened through the bridge does not reach a child
// @stdout PASS a copy carries the permission bits on the systems that have them
// @stdout PASS a copy of the permissions of nothing reports why
// @stdout PASS a name outside the system code page survives every bridge
// @stdout 32/32 tests passed
// The bridges std.fs reads and changes a path through, exercised on the host running the suite. A
// structure field read at a wrong offset is what these tests exist to catch, since two of the three
// seed hosts cannot be run by hand.

main() -> int {
    return 0;
}
